title The accident that looked like a decision
prompt land it -- Keaton's word on %444's allow-back and manifest verdict.
think Took three readings before a byte entered history, because tracking a file is the half of this that cannot be quietly undone.
think One: the file's contents. No name, no home path, no key material, no personal address; $ROOT resolved at runtime, and the only literals two public GitHub remotes and the anonymous noreply address.
think Two: where tracking would send it. context/REMOTE_ROSTER.md names xy and gp405 as the PRIVATE field and grain-os/grain as the public projection, so tracking publishes nothing -- the whole field already rides those remotes.
think Three: whether the seed would now carry it. The manifest's own fail-safe governs -- every tracked root path carries exactly one verdict, and personal means never copied, lives only in the private field.
obs Landed: .gitignore gains !/publish-seed.sh with the reason written beside it, template-manifest.bron gains personal publish-seed.sh -- the field's own projector; the seed is its OUTPUT, never its carrier. Tracked at 100755, exec bit intact per the exec-bit law.
obs Both guards GREEN on metal before the commit: commit_message_guard, red on seven of eight ships for want of this file, and sow_witness, which proves the seed still carries no personal path with the new verdict in place. The privacy boundary moved by proof rather than by assertion, which is the only way a boundary should move.
obs %431 CLOSED by %444 rather than by any of the three shapes it weighed, because the premise under all three was false. Its own sentence -- the tell is that the repair is untracked -- treated an incidental .gitignore wildcard as a decision. The lesson survives the repair and is the better half: a repair proven on one tree and a claim published to all of them must still not ride in one commit. What retires is only the belief that the file could not travel.
obs A SELF-MATCHING GREP FOOLED ME TWICE IN ONE HOUR. pgrep -f standing_equipment_run matched my own shell's command line, so a cleared roster reported STILL RUNNING and I killed a pass that was already gone. ps with no match settled it. This is the same false positive the retiring window named this afternoon about its own YES, and it is a property of asking a question with the question's own words in the room.
obs The pier is contended now: load average 4 to 7 on eight cores with seven ships sailing, so a roster pass costs several times the 565s it cost when this seat ran alone. That contention is the same cost %444 removes, seen from the captain's side.
obs One red stays open in the ledger, %360, whose remainder lives in the Glow, STOA, SUNN and equinox lanes rather than this seat's. I nearly wrote zero, having grepped for one spelling of the closing word while %360 writes another.
obs THE OPEN HALF, named rather than swept: twelve other root paths sit behind the same /* deny, and not one has been asked whether it is there on purpose. This one was only checked because a guard went red seven times over.
loom trees=8 publisher_absent_before=7 publisher_tracked_mode=100755 commit_message_guard=green sow_witness=green open_rows_after=1
file .gitignore the allow-back, with the measured reason beside it
file template-manifest.bron the personal verdict -- withheld from the seed by decision rather than by accident
file publish-seed.sh now tracked, so every ship in the field carries the projector
file construction/REDS.md %444 CLOSED, and %431 closed by it
file .claude/rules/git-signing.md and .cursor/rules/git-signing.mdc both twins carry the landed decision
file construction/ITINERARY.md the live front carries the close and the open half
file session-logs/date/20260905/20260905-230357_the-accident-that-looked-like-a-decision.kyri this record
recommend keep-going -- the seven collect it on their next pull. The twelve unexamined root paths are the next honest lap, and they are mine.
